import{_ as t,o,c as r,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Team Project","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"project/README.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"project/index.md","filePath":"project/index.md"}'),s={name:"project/index.md"};function n(i,e,d,h,l,c){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="the-team-project" tabindex="-1">The Team Project <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-team-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Team Project&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>A semester of real engineering in a real open-source codebase: in teams of 3–4, you specify a contribution, delegate parts of it to agents, verify it to a standard a maintainer would accept, and defend it at the end. You are graded on that process, never on whether a stranger merges your code.</p></blockquote><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Weeks</strong></td><td>2 to 16 (finals week). Teams form in week 2; the final presentations are in the final exam period</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Teams</strong></td><td>3–4 students. CS [4xxx] and CS [5xxx] students may be on the same team, with the same team expectations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Codebase</strong></td><td><strong>The class codebase</strong>: a class fork of a real, active open-source project, chosen by the instructor before the term (<a href="./class-codebase.html">The Class Codebase</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Where you work</strong></td><td>Your <strong>team fork</strong>: your team&#39;s own fork of the class fork, in the course&#39;s GitHub organization</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milestones</strong></td><td>M0 proposal (end of week 5) · M1 first PR (end of week 8) · M2 major contribution (end of week 12) · Final (finals week): <a href="./milestones.html">Team Project Milestones</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graded as</strong></td><td>35% of the course for CS [4xxx], 30% for CS [5xxx] (<a href="./../assessment/rubrics.html#team-project-milestones">rubrics</a>); the PR round counts in Labs and assignments (<a href="./../assessment/rubrics.html#the-pr-round">rubric</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Related pages</strong></td><td><a href="./contribution-rules.html">Contribution rules</a> · <a href="./pr-round.html">The PR round</a> · <a href="./final-presentation.html">The final presentation</a> · <a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html">Agent safety contract</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-three-tiers" tabindex="-1">The three tiers <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-three-tiers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The three tiers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The design follows what comparable courses learned. CMU&#39;s 17-313 builds on a class fork of a real agent codebase and grades &quot;a snapshot of your repository (including open branches and PRs)&quot; (<a href="https://cmu-313.github.io/assignments/P2/2_firstsprint/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">17-313</a>). UT Austin&#39;s CS 378 runs a peer PR round in classmates&#39; repositories (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>). And Michigan&#39;s EECS 481, after five years of student PRs to outside projects (44% accepted), advises courses to &quot;avoid mandatory grading of whether pull requests are accepted&quot; (<a href="https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~weimerw/p/weimer-fse2023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Fang et al.</a>). So:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>What you do</th><th>When</th><th>Graded as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. Core work in your team fork</strong></td><td>Your team picks a feature area and issues in the class codebase, specifies them, and builds them through PRs to the team fork, each reviewed by a teammate and checked by CI. Staff grade a snapshot of the fork at each deadline</td><td>Weeks 2–16</td><td>M0, M1, M2 and Final</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. The PR round</strong></td><td>Every student opens one PR on another team&#39;s fork; the receiving team reviews it and decides</td><td>Weeks 9–10</td><td>Individually, in Labs and assignments (<a href="./pr-round.html">The PR Round</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Optional upstream PRs</strong></td><td>A change already reviewed in your team fork, offered to the parent project (or a short pre-vetted list), only where that project&#39;s written policy allows disclosed AI assistance</td><td>Weeks 12–15</td><td>A small bonus if merged, never a penalty (<a href="./contribution-rules.html">Contribution Rules</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Tier 1 is where almost all of the grade comes from, and it depends on nobody outside the course. Tier 3 is a real-world bonus with real-world rules: maintainers in 2026 are overloaded with AI-generated pull requests, and the course&#39;s reputation, and the University&#39;s, rides on each one.</p><h2 id="teams" tabindex="-1">Teams <a class="header-anchor" href="#teams" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Teams&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Forming teams.</strong> In week 1 you fill in a short Canvas survey: your languages, your weekly availability, the kind of work you want (core logic, tests and CI, tooling, documentation), and up to one person you&#39;d like to work with. The instructor forms teams of 3–4 and announces them in week 2, balancing experience and schedules.</li><li><strong>The team charter.</strong> In week 2 your team writes <code>course/TEAM_CHARTER.md</code> (<a href="./../templates/TEAM_CHARTER.html">template</a>): how you communicate, when you meet, how you split and review work, what &quot;done&quot; means, and what you do when someone falls behind or you disagree. Keep it current; M0 checks it.</li><li><strong>Rotating roles.</strong> For each milestone, one member is the <strong>integrator</strong> (keeps <code>main</code> green, merges approved PRs, runs the milestone checklist) and one is the <strong>verifier</strong> (owns the CI gates and the mutation and eval numbers). Rotate them, so everyone does each at least once.</li><li><strong>Graduate students</strong> on a team do the same team work as everyone else; their graduate work is separate (<a href="./../graduate/">graduate/</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="how-the-work-flows" tabindex="-1">How the work flows <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-work-flows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the work flows&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every change goes through the same path, which is also how most open-source projects work:</p><ol><li><strong>An issue</strong> in your team fork, with a &quot;done when&quot; and an owner, linked from <code>course/TASKS.md</code>.</li><li><strong>Specify · Delegate · Verify</strong> in <code>course/AI_LOG.md</code> before you start: what&#39;s specified, what an agent will do with which permissions, how you&#39;ll check it.</li><li><strong>A branch</strong> named <code>[github-username]/[issue-number]-[short-name]</code>, worked in the Safe Loop: small steps, tests, reading every diff.</li><li><strong>A PR</strong> to the team fork&#39;s <code>main</code>, with a description you write yourself (🔴 AI off) and the AI disclosure section filled in (<a href="./../templates/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.html">PR template</a>). Commits made with AI help end with <code>Assisted-by: [tool and model]</code>.</li><li><strong>CI</strong> runs the project&#39;s tests (and, from week 9, lint and CodeQL or Semgrep). A red build is not ready for review.</li><li><strong>A teammate&#39;s review</strong> (<a href="./../templates/REVIEW.html">REVIEW template</a>), with located comments; you answer every one with a change or a reason.</li><li><strong>Merge</strong> by the integrator, once the review approves and CI is green.</li></ol><h3 id="where-things-live" tabindex="-1">Where things live <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-things-live" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where things live&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your team fork is a copy of someone else&#39;s project, so keep the course&#39;s documents out of the project&#39;s own files. That way any change can later go upstream (Tier 3) without dragging course paperwork along.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where in the team fork</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The project&#39;s own code, tests and docs</td><td>Where the project keeps them</td></tr><tr><td><code>AGENTS.md</code> (instructions for agents working in this fork)</td><td>The repository root, where agents look for it. If the project already has one, add a clearly marked course section instead of replacing it</td></tr><tr><td>The team&#39;s course documents (listed below)</td><td>A <code>course/</code> folder at the root. Those with a template in <code>templates/</code> start from it (for example <a href="./../templates/SPEC.html">SPEC.md template</a> and <a href="./../templates/AI_LOG.html">AI_LOG.md template</a>); the others follow the outline in their week&#39;s lab</td></tr><tr><td>CI for the course&#39;s gates</td><td>The fork&#39;s workflow files, kept separate from the project&#39;s own workflows where possible (<code>.github/workflows/course-gates.yml</code>, week 9)</td></tr><tr><td>Grades, peer evaluations, reflections, the final report (its draft in week 14, the report in finals week)</td><td><strong>Canvas only</strong>, never the fork</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The files in <code>course/</code>, by the week that starts them:</p><ul><li><code>course/TEAM_CHARTER.md</code>: week 2.</li><li><code>course/THREAT_MODEL.md</code>: week 4, updated in week 10.</li><li><code>course/SPEC.md</code>, <code>course/DESIGN.md</code> and <code>course/TASKS.md</code>: M0 (week 5), kept current to the Final.</li><li><code>course/AI_LOG.md</code>: from the team&#39;s first task on, a Specify · Delegate · Verify entry for each change (step 2 above).</li><li><code>course/SECURITY_REVIEW.md</code>: week 10.</li><li><code>course/EVAL_PLAN.md</code>: M2 (weeks 11–12), if your contribution uses an LLM.</li><li><code>course/RETROSPECTIVE.md</code>, with its data and script in <code>course/data/</code>: week 13.</li></ul><p>Your <strong>onboarding report</strong> (week 2) is individual, so it isn&#39;t in the team fork: it goes in <code>course/ONBOARDING.md</code>, with the agent&#39;s map in <code>course/architecture-map.agent.md</code>, in <strong>your own fork</strong> of the class fork (<a href="./../weeks/02-inside-agents/lab.html#assignment--the-onboarding-report">week 2</a>).</p><h2 id="agents-in-the-team-fork" tabindex="-1">Agents in the team fork <a class="header-anchor" href="#agents-in-the-team-fork" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agents in the team fork&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html">agent safety contract</a> applies in full. In the team fork it means:</p><ul><li><strong>Work in a Codespace or a dev container</strong>, with approvals on. The class codebase is someone else&#39;s code: open it with an agent only in a sandbox.</li><li><strong>Agents never push to <code>main</code>.</strong> They work on your branch; you open the PR and you write its description.</li><li><strong>Tokens are narrow:</strong> fine-grained, for your team fork only, short-lived. No agent gets a token that can reach another team&#39;s fork or the upstream project.</li><li><strong>Background and cloud agents</strong> (week 13) run only on your team fork or your own fork, never against another team&#39;s fork or upstream, and every PR they draft is reviewed by you before anyone else sees it.</li><li><strong>No unattended posting.</strong> No agent opens issues, comments or PRs in another team&#39;s fork or upstream on its own.</li><li><strong>The data rule:</strong> the class codebase is public open-source code, so free tiers are fine here. Personal data and secrets never go into a prompt.</li></ul><h2 id="how-its-graded" tabindex="-1">How it&#39;s graded <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-its-graded" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How it&#39;s graded&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Milestones</strong> M0 (15 points), M1 (30), M2 (40) and Final (65) have team rows and individual rows (<a href="./../assessment/rubrics.html#team-project-milestones">rubrics</a>). The PR round (20) is individual.</li><li><strong>Snapshots.</strong> At each deadline staff tag your fork&#39;s <code>main</code> (<code>m0</code>, <code>m1</code>, <code>m2</code>, and <code>final</code> at the start of the final exam period), record the open branches and PRs, and grade that. Open, reviewed PRs count; nothing has to be merged upstream.</li><li><strong>Peer evaluations</strong> on Canvas with M1, M2 and Final, together with the contribution evidence in the fork, can adjust a member&#39;s share of the team rows (<a href="./../assessment/#team-grades-and-peer-evaluation">how</a>).</li><li><strong>The final presentation</strong> (finals week) is a ten-minute team presentation with a live demo (<a href="./final-presentation.html">The Final Presentation</a>). There is no final examination.</li><li><strong>What isn&#39;t graded:</strong> lines of code, number of PRs, how much an agent did, tokens spent, and whether anyone upstream merged anything.</li></ul><h2 id="ai-use-on-the-team-project" tabindex="-1">AI use on the team project <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-use-on-the-team-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI use on the team project&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>On the team project</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>🟢 <strong>AI on</strong></td><td>Code, tests, refactors, exploring the codebase, CI configuration, AI reviewers running alongside your own review</td></tr><tr><td>🟡 <strong>AI limited</strong></td><td><code>SPEC.md</code> (you write the criteria; an AI may critique them), <code>DESIGN.md</code> (an agent may explore and propose options; the decisions are yours), <code>TASKS.md</code> and <code>AGENTS.md</code> (you write them; an AI may critique them), <code>THREAT_MODEL.md</code> (an AI may brainstorm attacks; the analysis is yours), and the final report (AI may help with tables, figures and checking claims against the fork; the limitations, reflection and contribution sections are written by your team, 🔴)</td></tr><tr><td>🔴 <strong>AI off</strong></td><td>PR descriptions and review comments you sign; peer evaluations; the reflection parts of the final report</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The complete list is in the <a href="./../assessment/#ai-use-policy">AI use policy</a>. Where the parent project&#39;s AI policy is stricter, it wins (<a href="./contribution-rules.html">contribution rules</a>).</p><h2 id="if-things-go-wrong" tabindex="-1">If things go wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-things-go-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If things go wrong&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The build or the test suite breaks after a sync:</strong> tell the instructor; staff fix the class fork and announce a known-good commit. Don&#39;t pull from upstream yourselves.</li><li><strong>The codebase is harder than your plan assumed:</strong> shrink the scope at the next milestone and say why in <code>course/TASKS.md</code>. A smaller, verified contribution beats a larger, unverified one, and the rubric says so.</li><li><strong>An agent did something you didn&#39;t approve</strong> (pushed, deleted, posted): stop, revoke its token, write down what happened in <code>course/AI_LOG.md</code>, and tell the instructor. Reporting it is never penalized.</li><li><strong>A teammate has gone quiet:</strong> use your charter&#39;s process first (a direct message, then a team conversation). If that doesn&#39;t work within a week, tell the instructor. Don&#39;t wait for the peer evaluation.</li><li><strong>You&#39;re out of free credits:</strong> switch to a fallback or the lab machine (<a href="./../resources/troubleshooting.html">troubleshooting</a>). It isn&#39;t a reason for a late milestone.</li></ul>',28)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,g as default};
